#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e7f341-ca58-50f5-b31a-bc4df196467b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122511} {:answer/id #uuid "8c230bdc-4f5d-5cad-9f5c-b5b401d42add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122512} {:answer/id #uuid "926108c6-a205-5d17-ae13-42d2b2777d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2} - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122513} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1e438f-776b-5020-a2f7-6188b2fb2d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122514} {:answer/id #uuid "7805d382-87cf-585f-b4e4-fff5eececbde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2} + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122515}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5a4f19a2-5e11-5b0d-8c0b-47dd589bd321" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122510} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "846ef2ef-efaf-5373-90be-dae978b85bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379798} {:answer/id #uuid "52c85121-2c18-50db-baeb-702e74670930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379799} {:answer/id #uuid "7a960889-125f-5a3d-bad2-a8bc9c217086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379800} {:answer/id #uuid "0073597d-246f-5dcc-97ed-8edc25c32397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379801} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa04b63-3f0a-57e3-a466-af908cf8a8cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0073597d-246f-5dcc-97ed-8edc25c32397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379801} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4bc4821-7fbf-5b60-85e0-87169f6c99b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5ad66c9-62da-59fa-88c0-70fb1c8458db" :question/math-academy-id "q-36434" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(1,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =\\frac{1}{2-{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(1,2)$ is the $1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =y-{x}^{2} \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(1,2)$ is the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0da5739-46c6-51dd-b114-d639bef88db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459877} {:answer/id #uuid "5c471f96-4c44-5667-8ab1-8ac4ada23253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0da5739-46c6-51dd-b114-d639bef88db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459877} :answer-field/id #uuid "4016c208-5132-52ca-9a94-45c02d928df9" :answer-field/key "$c=-1$ gives a line" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460805} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b030eebf-9f96-541c-90b5-e11292ebe597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459879} {:answer/id #uuid "99d9245a-b26b-504e-872d-b0683ba7eeff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99d9245a-b26b-504e-872d-b0683ba7eeff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459880} :answer-field/id #uuid "411d20a9-891c-5142-a326-3f8734eb565f" :answer-field/key "$c=0$ gives a parabola and the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460806} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c65a8236-1fb5-5f5f-9b24-1401e140dfcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459881} {:answer/id #uuid "a16c0f21-d8cc-5644-b977-7a9c0d956a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c65a8236-1fb5-5f5f-9b24-1401e140dfcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459881} :answer-field/id #uuid "791a2484-953d-5785-9ff6-1001a3e16b51" :answer-field/key "$c=1$ gives two lines" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "592215bc-9340-546e-9e8f-8c90c87cd2d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-36829" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}x$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}x & =c \\\\ xy({x}^{2}-y) & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=\\pm 1$ then the level curves are $xy({x}^{2}-y)=\\pm 1,$ which are not lines.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curves are $xy({x}^{2}-y)=0,$ which consists of the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$, and the parabola $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nSo, only statement “$c=0$ gives a parabola and the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbb4807-24a9-5fa7-a053-64ede7706481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/79d8db55b0de544c328188d2af444d848658b65626e753bdc1654791cc457506.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333129} {:answer/id #uuid "6510be84-880e-5d4f-804e-c57c775526c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6f16e785aaef98bf6609a4ef79b6574e3650110607680da5fdbb8034eb590457.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333908} {:answer/id #uuid "9a1f5f69-600f-50ee-bca5-b6a75b2877f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/65/6521da99a82e38a68a5aa87a0c7c88eacf4ed7b78351af594ec309fa2cb7f57b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334350} {:answer/id #uuid "b785490c-3fb9-5a57-894e-b521694b263e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/56e4941d265e1fc778e7b5672a14f38d45ba43b13562f3ddab53cebd5175cea4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334597} {:answer/id #uuid "d906d727-e913-509d-9cf4-4581970726ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4c/4c259d100b5e63a37822df6f7a5b5a6493efdb13b147a76a0183d120e6aeec5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a1f5f69-600f-50ee-bca5-b6a75b2877f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/65/6521da99a82e38a68a5aa87a0c7c88eacf4ed7b78351af594ec309fa2cb7f57b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334350} :answer-field/id #uuid "49202a10-c9c8-5356-be37-70bc7b8b058d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5348f454-8724-5136-a5f0-8c87b0d92c2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64908" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2{x}^{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2{x}^{2}) & =c \\\\ y-2{x}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ y & =2{x}^{2}+{e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of parabolas with the vertex at $(0,{e}^{c}).$ Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/65/6521da99a82e38a68a5aa87a0c7c88eacf4ed7b78351af594ec309fa2cb7f57b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128844 :question/id #uuid "c6495ab5-3756-50e6-8977-7ec19fba4466" :question/math-academy-id "e-11470" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2 + y^2 = 4$ for $y \\geq 0$ between the surfaces $z =0$ and $z = 10 - x.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R y \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9a/9ad0d507a5440cfce9595e9054457fd733ae412d9a57b0a85238b4773ff0a0c7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=10-x = 10 - r\\cos\\theta.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 10-r\\cos\\theta \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128845 :question/id #uuid "043cc93d-1eae-5640-a109-9acbe7a34f92" :question/math-academy-id "e-11471" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\, : \\, (x-1)^2 + y^2 \\leq 1, \\: -2 \\leq z \\leq 4-y \\right\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/a5/a551e379767395a2422f690a66fc38defab64cff03c752c0c9a5a0748561d2f7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z = -2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z = 4 - y = 4 - r\\sin{\\theta}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x-1)^2 + y^2 = 1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r = 2\\cos{\\theta}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 2\\cos{\\theta}, \\: -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: -2 \\leq z \\leq 4-r\\sin{\\theta} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{4-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128853 :question/id #uuid "e46cfe46-965d-5c28-90ed-060ec39751a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12376" :question/problem "Given that $D = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\, : \\, x^2 + (y+2)^2 \\leq 4\\right\\},$ express the following integral in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D e^{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}A\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D{:}$\n\n![](images/e4/e447e688e0f3e2c0411129e8d1baf4a900008173443f5392d98af52a27eb285b.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n$$\n\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = r \\cos   \\theta  \\\\ y = r \\sin   \\theta \\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^2 + (y+2)^2 = 4$ in polar coordinates:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {x}^{2} + ( y + 2 {)}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 4 \\\\ \\displaystyle {( r \\cos   \\theta  )}^{2} + {( r \\sin   \\theta  + 2 )}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 4 \\\\ \\displaystyle {r}^{2} {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  + {r}^{2} {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  + 4 r \\sin   \\theta  + 4 & \\displaystyle  = 4 \\\\ \\displaystyle {r}^{2} ( {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  + {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  ) + 4 r \\sin   \\theta  & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle {r}^{2} + 4 r \\sin   \\theta  & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle r ( r + 4 \\sin   \\theta  ) & \\displaystyle  = 0\\end{array}\n$$ \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r = -4\\sin{\\theta}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r = -4\\sin{\\theta}$ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/52/522df0e95e36cd67f34ffcc0ec4757c35749eab5d5137215c456c8b7e90994ae.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta = \\pi$ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta  = 2 \\pi $ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/6a/6ad24b638877941296df25869f8b0ee6f9dc1e73a779a8a0da01de35d9f74f42.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq -4\\sin{\\theta}, \\, \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\underset{D}{\\iint } {e}^{\\sqrt{ {x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{d} A & \\displaystyle  = \\underset{\\mathrm{\\Delta }}{\\iint } {e}^{\\sqrt{( r \\cos   \\theta  {)}^{2} + ( r \\sin   \\theta  {)}^{2}}} \\hspace{0.167em} r \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{d} r \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{d} \\theta  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2 \\pi } {\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin   \\theta } r {e}^{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} ( {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  + {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  )}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{d} r \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{d} \\theta  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2 \\pi } {\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin   \\theta } r {e}^{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2}}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{d} r \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{d} \\theta  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2 \\pi } {\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin   \\theta } r {e}^{r} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{d} r \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{d} \\theta  .\\end{array}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128856 :question/id #uuid "a1b2e13a-95cd-5e0f-aa97-c3e90433560c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12402" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x+2y \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=5$ for $x\\geq 0,$ $y\\geq 0$ and between the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=y+3.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b7/b7b37ffe8e97ed4a661ee98750894641c6ed3354864dd5b54d2ed076e4fdb69b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\sin\\theta,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y+3=r\\sin\\theta + 3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq \\sqrt{5}, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: r\\sin\\theta \\leq z \\leq r\\sin\\theta + 3\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x+2y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )[z]_{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}3{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )]_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{5}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{(\\sqrt{5})}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5\\sqrt{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}(1-0)-10\\sqrt{5}(0-1) \\\\  & =15\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129016 :question/id #uuid "d3ae6c85-14bd-57ad-b8cc-1cebb53683b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7346" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/8} \\int_0^{\\sin2\\theta} r \\, \\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{[{r}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} = 1-2\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin^22\\theta = \\dfrac12\\left(1-\\cos4\\theta\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))-(0-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{2}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}(\\pi -2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129017 :question/id #uuid "059deff9-dfa3-5c3e-88b1-194af41d3ed0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7347" :question/problem "![](images/17/1782385c55493892be0308ede1e4fbe5308ea9440e21b70cc2da7d7f01fb8dcd.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D y \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129018 :question/id #uuid "f31dd085-70fa-5eed-adf9-89fb0e6654e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7348" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x, y) = 15xy^2$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $x \\geq 0,y \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/05/051cfbbf3fd37f01ff7e4442fccdc5ec4ce506e3c452b68bfa2ed5ae6e5cf6b9.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:0\\leq \\theta \\leq  \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}15x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}15r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({2}^{5}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32({sin}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-{sin}^{3}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =32(1-0) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94118c9f-f252-5126-95dc-39997a9303de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{5}r^{3}\\cos θ\\sin θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126573} {:answer/id #uuid "a19a57f2-5551-5378-ae40-8fe5a86f9afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}r^{3}\\cos θ\\sin θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126574} {:answer/id #uuid "bb19ef63-baf8-585f-b58d-ebdee10fd1f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{5}r^{4}\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126575} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d69333-7a0c-54e6-81f2-56b5cc028849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}r^{4}\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126576} {:answer/id #uuid "4efd860c-2bdc-522c-a4aa-77ae001cab6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}r^{4}\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126577}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2f5d71c0-1eda-5098-a773-eb6dafd40de1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126572}] :question/id #uuid "96701d26-3867-5d84-962d-70db90473eae" :question/math-academy-id "q-161044" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2fe61de100b4a469e4d79dc67869dd68936a0bf396757c4d08fb4d6cade6bda.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)xy^{2}dA\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b681cf26-ad41-5653-bce9-580974381de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6\\cos θ}r^{2}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126579} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4598f3-014d-5222-9a41-c4efd38e3ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}r^{3}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126580} {:answer/id #uuid "e88b4c4a-b486-568a-b20f-a07fa3b118c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6\\cos θ}r^{3}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126581} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6bf261-6b14-56f1-8c08-c0943ec14aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}r^{3}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126582} {:answer/id #uuid "3f871b80-da7e-53c9-96ef-432946627099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6\\cos θ}r^{3}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126583}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2356402a-db2e-5c19-a235-969c763d3490" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126578}] :question/id #uuid "46644fa9-6cbb-532b-913d-789dd58285a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161068" :question/problem "If $D = {(x, y): (x - 3)^{2} + y^{2} \\le 9}$, then $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)(x^{2} - y^{2})dA =\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19edc691-38d0-5ad2-be38-1638ded6e729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555048} {:answer/id #uuid "6e67f273-e80f-584c-a56c-d08f5c55b249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555253} {:answer/id #uuid "bd707b9b-7e24-5f86-9393-637c67c6bcee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555451} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1bd12e-1cd5-5a17-a462-56e0ee79d849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555491} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a983f6-7f59-5aad-b3c8-497270d02f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3a983f6-7f59-5aad-b3c8-497270d02f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555557} :answer-field/id #uuid "5659b1e7-edd6-5858-a187-e1aacd269a66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a6db462-1cdd-5328-ac3f-82ce0a5c5a14" :question/math-academy-id "q-161520" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$  for $x\\ge 0$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=6+y.$\nExpress the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xz\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b1/b194b4882dcada56a10ec69f9dae80b363ea05efaae27385ceacc9a15ba3b56f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $5$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6+y=6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xz\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )z\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "267e1e75-5ebd-55ab-842b-e40b9ba9c3d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555079} {:answer/id #uuid "5c988fe0-3022-55d5-a9a9-c2db39cbe076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2+r}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555207} {:answer/id #uuid "a295bb42-7a73-51b6-8777-f5e3a19c436a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555390} {:answer/id #uuid "d507184c-2f44-5f85-9623-216dcd8ab2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2+r}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555505} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e5d412-e590-5a31-80ed-967fe6f3ff74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1e5d412-e590-5a31-80ed-967fe6f3ff74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555595} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7d66d94-89fa-5367-8f41-89e27a901a29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0593d504-dbf8-59c9-9282-2775ebdf4b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-161521" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\le 9,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}xy\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/75/75bec31176c369e69d5b11af3eeab23f86a4f32251e37f56b225e9dd0bd4a3de.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2+{r}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +9 & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+{r}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd34198-353c-5d77-a4f3-b94aae48f5d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\sqrt{5}π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126609} {:answer/id #uuid "49514054-bbe2-5c52-9a41-dcbaab2d3878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126610} {:answer/id #uuid "6a5c8b4c-ae34-547e-bbaf-d473c39d68e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126611} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff3ab5b-66ac-584e-92cb-c1dbfb7e4d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{2}π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126612} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4a8acc-25d6-5523-b17f-7de7fd3724d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126613}] :answer-field/id #uuid "67803ba5-1ff4-58f3-9657-95238a5eb88b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "172f6194-d227-5c4b-a475-2c378a3312df" :question/math-academy-id "q-161522" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∭_(R)\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dV\n$$\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$ and between the surfaces $z = 0$ and $z = 12 - 4y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17bc181a-8974-5f88-9734-198cc3d71ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564976} {:answer/id #uuid "26efd65d-e4b0-5765-a52f-7e5570d50b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565000} {:answer/id #uuid "3049d143-915e-5318-81f0-56825c644df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565010} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee7a4ae-58f1-55dc-93b1-0b065f6a2752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565131} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c377e8-f5a2-5500-8cd7-722a46d1c396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3049d143-915e-5318-81f0-56825c644df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565010} :answer-field/id #uuid "5de2e9ed-6a8e-5e65-b0f4-c004ab1d9bd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc7985de-f7b8-5b62-b788-d731ea9f3ce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161523" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/60/603dc3142d8329fd9ff9fe8d09b131fe09497405f761918d59261fde314772c6.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7aa44b-cefc-5820-8f30-d5e2344ef950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321243} {:answer/id #uuid "296bd36f-2f4a-5195-8d23-67afa8466ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321469} {:answer/id #uuid "5994704b-000c-507d-a814-14894c25981b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321854} {:answer/id #uuid "663ac189-5c11-5931-88ff-1f3a097d9de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321939} {:answer/id #uuid "eacb236a-ca73-5bff-8d2f-ea5761a80a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eacb236a-ca73-5bff-8d2f-ea5761a80a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322985} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f3bb291-72e9-573f-8060-c17a7f541fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fcddcef-1856-5848-a572-712ca0d53c78" :question/math-academy-id "q-38031" :question/problem "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,[{r}^{2}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c4a273a-2b19-5303-a5a5-11019a9369f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321746} {:answer/id #uuid "872c79ef-16ea-5094-9ae9-66db9b44bd25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322219} {:answer/id #uuid "93aa25f5-4394-5894-a3d6-3a8ed7446150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322320} {:answer/id #uuid "99c28fe9-f00e-52ea-be86-9376faf9d769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322369} {:answer/id #uuid "d2de48da-46c8-5206-a5b5-40a93d82b30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c4a273a-2b19-5303-a5a5-11019a9369f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321746} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c23bd6d-2dad-5455-a325-95bbab5758ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "695450ef-6209-5a8e-874a-0e67f88a5889" :question/math-academy-id "q-38076" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bccbe3973fa63908f5e264f4566e1207c5019dc1de9c36ef42481428527aef3b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the semicircle shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88c5f797-a9d1-57fa-9b5c-199c1707c699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\cos θ\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2\\cos θ}r^{3}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127250} {:answer/id #uuid "a5444d7b-d73e-51e7-acb5-9a93ee60e11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\cos θ\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2\\cos θ}r^{2}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127251} {:answer/id #uuid "bae770e3-6a1c-54a7-b9f0-d75d08314408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos θ\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}r^{2}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127252} {:answer/id #uuid "65798862-d116-58c2-b3af-89c7218b8ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos θ\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}rdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127253} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb4e9b6-29bc-533d-854e-c1558288c702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos θ\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2\\cos θ}r^{3}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127254}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b1a553d3-061a-5f0f-8a06-f47ae65fdaea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127249}] :question/id #uuid "e8b65659-f286-503b-84ce-d0959f9a4ff0" :question/math-academy-id "q-38077" :question/problem "If $D = {(x, y): (x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1, y \\ge 0}$, then $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)x\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA =\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acba3f29-0158-52f0-9456-91c5be61af4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127268} {:answer/id #uuid "b65a27eb-39be-5bb5-a399-1d44b7927674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127269} {:answer/id #uuid "dacc8225-ad29-548c-99c4-034d05c1b2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127270} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a0d310-0000-5652-8ada-f073e31a559d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127271} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc4c481-03e9-5e2b-b660-a09ea114f9c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127272}] :answer-field/id #uuid "74727eb8-963c-5008-8d8c-d65c40ef415d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127267}] :question/id #uuid "4de8b5f4-2e7e-535e-81ad-97991889d6bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-38127" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{π/3}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r^{2}\\sin θdrdθ\n$$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dbb1193-47be-5246-a935-a3e2f7a5d9cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321372} {:answer/id #uuid "6be44563-7ff5-5aff-888b-8534ab2d8574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321988} {:answer/id #uuid "7d81d17b-e0e4-5462-aa1e-2daeda378c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322134} {:answer/id #uuid "9e663173-e8bf-5e07-9a64-eabd853d519d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322395} {:answer/id #uuid "c307b8a7-c480-5cfb-a7fb-ea588c87cd94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c307b8a7-c480-5cfb-a7fb-ea588c87cd94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322662} :answer-field/id #uuid "54b4bbfb-a31e-55b3-9e9c-d9e99a687a4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a604ede7-8a36-5960-82df-176ca647b425" :question/math-academy-id "q-38140" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=2x+y$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/b5/b5ba69ab02aa13e201b7b9113f6a36e705ad19e6412499504afc4f239459a8f3.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94aadad7-3353-5170-a5a1-d94e0dd91e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{10 - r cos ⁡ θ}r zdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127916} {:answer/id #uuid "a25dfbc8-54b3-5802-b238-d346d2326ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{10}r 2 zdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127917} {:answer/id #uuid "dd096352-52ff-520a-9256-99c4b17974ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{10 - r cos ⁡ θ}r 2 zdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127918} {:answer/id #uuid "0a850283-aa76-5b31-a337-1ad320968c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{9}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{10 - r sin ⁡ θ}r 2 zdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127919} {:answer/id #uuid "494dcffc-925c-5deb-be37-3c33e9b46f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{9}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{10}r 2 zdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127920}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ef5d7669-0b63-5970-8efc-622f5c6b1a62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127915} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7534b4b-0be3-57d1-9602-f5788953a87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10}{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368536} {:answer/id #uuid "83bc9502-bd0e-55c2-88f7-c99dfeb46a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368537} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa867b2-61b8-564d-bf6d-b7e1d2f94971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368538} {:answer/id #uuid "35e7552c-f179-58ed-93c4-67028d32cb70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\int }_{1}^{10-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368539} {:answer/id #uuid "eb28f31a-ad06-547b-a0a7-98b1ef8b5e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\int }_{1}^{10}{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fa867b2-61b8-564d-bf6d-b7e1d2f94971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368538} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1676e97-4bf4-5377-a977-58856345b080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d9d79e2-4f89-52ac-a84b-fb5b127d47eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-39481" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ between the surfaces $z=1$ and $z=10-x.$\nExpress the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9c/9c4ea0ba92e19a201e3944defa6deb0bc1ee76971adaa809521722b6ac31241f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=10-x=10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,1\\le z\\le 10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}z\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}z\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129767 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b933786-9d3d-57c2-84f4-835643209b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555094} {:answer/id #uuid "5e342a92-b054-5f7f-89ee-488cce153f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555212} {:answer/id #uuid "68b68f0f-6620-5a61-979c-32fad52a95ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555236} {:answer/id #uuid "98a247f0-d378-52a2-8196-59d5ace42597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555368} {:answer/id #uuid "c25b0420-ae5a-5adf-91a4-f4c1d49d88ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98a247f0-d378-52a2-8196-59d5ace42597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555368} :answer-field/id #uuid "d042f8c3-3f5c-5724-9b36-795b04d59a4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c05e88d4-386c-59e5-8052-d6f2602a779c" :question/math-academy-id "q-40785" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2z\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$  for $y\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=-x$ and $z=2+x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/06/063fafc4f8c50dba56bcfb50b8366a2ffb8c713f5668ce22d10c0370376bbaba.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-x=-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2+x=2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le 2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2z\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }2rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r[{z}^{2}]_{z=-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{z=2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r((2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r+4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{[2{r}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2(3)^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}(3)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }18+36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[18\\theta +36\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi } \\\\  & =18(\\pi -0)+36(0-0) \\\\  & =18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd51e610-6f03-5601-be97-90a082867f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128804} {:answer/id #uuid "115c217e-8e0a-5c5b-8302-ae521adc07f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128805} {:answer/id #uuid "232b05d7-2819-5154-96f7-710edeff9ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128806} {:answer/id #uuid "4505c771-cef0-508f-89e7-d4849ae94ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128807} {:answer/id #uuid "6325e896-5085-50b5-ab25-23a0ebf6535e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd51e610-6f03-5601-be97-90a082867f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128804} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8292c76-a1e0-5537-aaa6-9b5b73963ff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "929376bc-8d45-5684-ae94-b99b8cb09d33" :question/math-academy-id "q-83968" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at \n$(0,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-{y}^{2}-y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({sin}^{5}⁡0-{sin}^{5}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133489 :question/id #uuid "03c75b64-65f4-5e12-b7b1-fa8b61893eb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12447" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z )\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k},$ express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =1+z+0 \\\\  & =z+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,z+1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133490 :question/id #uuid "9bb760a6-9060-532a-a59d-3cedf335daac" :question/math-academy-id "e-12448" :question/problem "Consider the positively oriented surface $S$ given by $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = 2x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4) \\\\  & =2+1+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133491 :question/id #uuid "dfba5156-6b5f-5622-9406-7598ae2630b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12449" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2{x}^{2}y+4{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133692 :question/id #uuid "78e7cbb2-d207-5d5e-bf46-5c3b2686933f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8740" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=\\ln(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ x^2e^{3y}\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla \\cdot\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{3y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+y}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0) & =\\frac{2}{2(1)+0}+3(1)^{2}{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133693 :question/id #uuid "4e4236a9-d99f-50b1-803a-ab3aea5a673e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8743" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j} - 2z\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point  $\\left(-1,0,2\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}+R\\,\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm {curl}\\, \\mathbf F =\\nabla\\times\\mathbf{F}=\\left| {\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  z}} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{array}} \\right|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ y & {x}^{2} & -2z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2})]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-0)j+(2x-1)k \\\\  & =(2x-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm {curl}\\, \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $\\left(-1,0,2\\right),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(-1,0,2) & =(2(-1)-1)\\,k=-3\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133696 :question/id #uuid "ac6e7a2c-19a3-595a-ba46-f38c653a9bec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8927" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d37f2a445986b2aae60603fed3bef1b480d8f049dfe17a0cf26325ac2eb167d8.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F?$\n\nI. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$\n\nII. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F <                    0$\n\nIV. $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{div}\\, \\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F > 0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F <         0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F =0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F,$ we say that $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $\\mathbf F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}]}
